### JS flow

```js
const pendingCards = new Map();

function onToolRequest(ev){
  const card = renderToolCard(ev.data);
  appendMessage(card, 'tool');
  pendingCards.set(ev.data.call_id, card);
}

function renderToolCard(data){
  const el = document.createElement('div');
  el.className = 'tool-card';
  el.dataset.callId = data.call_id;
  el.innerHTML = `
    <div class="tool-header"><span class="tool-name">🔧 ${data.name}</span><span class="tool-badge">host tool</span></div>
    <pre class="tool-params">${formatParams(data.params)}</pre>
    <div class="tool-actions">
      <button class="btn allow">Allow</button>
      <button class="btn allow-once">Allow Once</button>
      <button class="btn deny">Deny</button>
    </div>`;
  el.querySelector('.allow').onclick = () => approve(data.call_id, 'allow');
  el.querySelector('.allow-once').onclick = () => approve(data.call_id, 'allow_once');
  el.querySelector('.deny').onclick = () => approve(data.call_id, 'deny');
  return el;
}

async function approve(callId, decision){
  const r = await fetch('/api/tools/approve', {
    method: 'POST', headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},
    body: JSON.stringify({ call_id: callId, decision }),
  });
  if (!r.ok) toast('approval failed');
}

function onToolResult(ev){
  const card = pendingCards.get(ev.data.call_id);
  if (card) card.replaceWith(renderToolResult(ev.data.result));
  else appendMessage(renderToolResult(ev.data.result), 'tool');
}

function renderToolResult(result){
  const ok = result.ok;
  const el = document.createElement('div');
  el.className = `tool-result ${ok ? 'ok' : 'err'}`;
  const body = ok
    ? (result.stdout || '(no output)') + (result.stderr ? '\n--- stderr ---\n' + result.stderr : '')
    : result.error || 'unknown error';
  el.innerHTML = `
    <div class="result-bar">
      <span class="status">${ok ? '✓' : '✗'} ${ok ? 'finished' : 'failed'} ${result.duration_ms ? 'in ' + result.duration_ms + 'ms' : ''}</span>
      <button class="copy">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <pre class="result-body">${escapeHtml(body)}</pre>`;
  return el;
}
```

## 5. Model/tool-request format

For reliable parsing, have the model emit tool requests as fenced JSON:

```json
{
  "tool_request": {
    "name": "terminal",
    "params": {"command": "echo hello from model"}
  }
}
```

The backend intercepts this in the streaming loop (or in a post-processing
step before sending to Ollama) and replaces it with the `tool_request` SSE
event. Do not let raw tool-request JSON leak into the rendered chat; it
looks broken to the user.

## 6. Permission persistence / policy modes

Three decision modes:

- **Allow** — grant this tool for the rest of the conversation (store in
  `approvedTools` keyed by tool name). Future calls of the same tool skip
  the card and execute immediately.
- **Allow Once** — execute this call only, then forget.
- **Deny** — reject and optionally remember (store in `deniedTools`) so the
  model isn't re-asked for the same tool repeatedly.

Persist the user's default policy in settings (`tool_policy: ask|auto|off`).

## Verification

1. `curl http://localhost:8123/api/tools/list` → returns JSON array.
2. `node -e "..."` test (see section 2) → bridge returns `ok: true`.
3. Start a chat, prompt: "run `echo tool permission works` on the host".
   Frontend shows a permission card; click **Allow Once**.
4. Result panel appears with `tool permission works\n`.
5. Check SQLite `messages` table — a `tool` role message (or `assistant`
   message containing the result summary) is persisted.

## Hermes background enhancer (related pattern)

If you want the WebChat to use a stronger Hermes agent in the background
and feed its result into the local model's context, see
`references/hermes-background-enhancer.md`.

## Pitfalls

- **Don't make the user wait with a blank screen while the tool runs.**
  The result panel should show a spinner immediately after approval, before
  the backend emits `tool_result`.
- **Don't stream model tokens while a tool request is pending.** Pause the
  upstream reader after emitting `tool_request`; resume only after the
  decision resolves. Otherwise the model keeps generating and the context
  becomes incoherent.
- **Tool results can be huge.** Cap the panel height (`max-height: 320px`)
  and offer a "Copy" button; don't dump 10MB of nmap output into the DOM.
- **One tool request at a time.** Queue subsequent requests until the
  pending one resolves, or the UI becomes a wall of permission cards.
- **Match the existing SSE parser.** If the WebChat already splits events
  on `\n\n`, add `tool_request` and `tool_result` alongside `token`,
  `thinking`, `done`, etc. Don't change the delimiter.

